A||||6||DecadenceR||Misc. Questions (thermostat, brake bleeding)||||1||
Z||000000||DecadenceR||07-13-2001||02:31 PM||||First, I heve seen people in here say to not run your motor without a thermostat. What problem can it cause? Could it have anything to do with my exhaust smoke? I need to install a thermostat, I haven't had one in since who knows when. Should  iget the 195 OEM, or the 180?<BR>I was told by smoeone in a brake shop that the best way to bleed your brakes was to open up the bleeder screws, one by one, in the usual order. But he said not to pump the brakes. Leave them all oen. By the time you get to the last screw, go back to the first one you opened, and tighten it back up, in the same order. Supposedly this works really well. Anyone know od this or try it?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SigPic.jpg">  <BR>AIM - DecadenceR<BR>"For though I cannot fly, I'm not content to crawl..."||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||07-13-2001||03:35 PM||||Without the thermostat, coolant is always circulating thru the engine. So, it never reaches it normal operating temp, and haveing a cold engine will cause premature wear. It also adds restriction in the cooling system, so the coolant can absorb more heat from the engine, and dissapate more heat thru the radiator.<P>Unless your engine is running ice cold all the time, this is not likly the cause of your smoke problems. <P>Go for the 180, its about as cool as you can run without having trouble with the ECM and open and closed loop modes.<P>No idea about the brake thing...||||reg||1
Z||000002||k2m4||07-13-2001||03:36 PM||||hey Mr deca, i dont think thats a good idea open up all those bleeder valves open especially doing it by your self. youll definitely need 2 people. I can evvision lots of air in your sysytem doing it the way you described. <P>As far as your thermostat...im running my car without one now only cause i removed it yesterday due to the fact it was bad . i have the new one in my hand right now but im running a 160 during the summer and the 180 in the winter.it only takes 2 mins to replace so the luxury is there to customize your "settings". <BR>good luck<P>by the way i have seen those self bleeder valves on the internet for self bleeding  costing around 8 bucks per valve. if your going to bleed your self do it that way<P><P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 2M4<BR>86 1/2 GT||||reg||13
Z||000003||k2m4||07-13-2001||03:39 PM||||oh yeah and i notice a huge difference in power when running the car without a thermo. It doesnt heat up all the way and stays in the open loop mode. TCC doesnt kick in , gas milage sucks ...i used a 1/4 tank of gas on a normal trip that never uses that much and my exhaust wrieks of sh@t...i can definitely tell <P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 2M4<BR>86 1/2 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||DecadenceR||07-13-2001||04:14 PM||||Thanks for he stat advice guys. I do have to get a new one. I just have to ge tto Pep-boys when there isn't a 20 minute wait because they only have on a cashier.<P>K2m4 - When I used to work at Firestone, we used to do that one at a time to gravity-bleed the new calipers we were putting on. I never heard of this method of bleding that this guy told me. but he sewars by it, so I was just curious. I usually bleed my brakes when my little bro is here to pump the pedal for me. <P>While I'm at it, anyone use those vaccum pump things for one person bleeding? They're like $25 - $30? I heard they're junk, but I would like some info since they're sold everwhere.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||07-13-2001||04:20 PM||||The vacuum pump bleeders are good in theory.  The trick is getting a good vacuum pump.  If the pump's crap, it won't work for crap.  I've always used the 2 person method, myself, with fine results.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||07-13-2001||07:25 PM||||I usually gravity bleed the system. takes a bit longer maybe but works just fine. Part of it depends on how much air there is and were. Mc replacement can often be a pain no matter which way you do it.<P>I would not open all of them at once. The MC will drain quicker than you think allot of the time. If the MC goes dry you've got a real pain in the ass on you hands. <P>Since you are going to bleed them....<P>before you start drain the MC tank with a turkey baster or something like that. Don't try to get every drop out of the openings to the MC. Then wipe up the bottom of the tank with a soft low lint rag. Then fill with new fluid.<P>Bleed until new fluid runs from each wheel. For better results, compress the front pistons into the calipers. (Do that with the bleeders open so fluid doesn't backup into the MC.<P>Won't get all the old fluid, but you'll get enough to make a diferance, especially up front. (OE Fiero rears are a pain to flush w/o taking them apart.)||||reg||1
